<article class="env-lemma" id="090S">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/090S">Lemma <span data-tag="090S">10.96.7</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. Let $I = (f_1, \ldots , f_ r)$ be a finitely generated ideal. If $M \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M/f_ i^ nM$ is surjective for each $i$, then $M \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M/I^ nM$ is surjective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Note that $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M/I^ nM = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M/(f_1^ n, \ldots , f_ r^ n)M$ as $I^ n \supset (f_1^ n, \ldots , f_ r^ n) \supset I^{rn}$. An element $\xi $ of $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M/(f_1^ n, \ldots , f_ r^ n)M$ can be symbolically written as 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xi = \sum \nolimits _{n \geq 0} \sum \nolimits _ i f_ i^ n x_{n, i}  \]
</div>
    <p>
       with $x_{n, i} \in M$. If $M \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M/f_ i^ nM$ is surjective, then there is an $x_ i \in M$ mapping to $\sum x_{n, i} f_ i^ n$ in $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M/f_ i^ nM$. Then $x = \sum x_ i$ maps to $\xi $ in $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M/I^ nM$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>